{
  "diagnosis": "The agent under-weighted the stated active condition and the patient's existing treatment in favor of surface symptoms, causing off-target or contraindicated choices; it also failed to use drug-class safety logic (route, systemic exposure, interaction with current therapy) and treated unrelated comorbidities as primary drivers.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "anchor_to_stated_condition",
      "text": "Before comparing options, restate the working diagnosis or the condition the visit is actually about, and require every candidate to have a plausible indication for that condition. If a candidate only treats a symptom, comorbidity, or side effect rather than the stated condition, rank it lower unless the question explicitly asks for symptomatic or preventive therapy."
    },
    {
      "id": "reconcile_current_medications",
      "text": "List the patient's current medicines and what each is treating, then check whether the proposed option duplicates, antagonizes, or compounds toxicity with any of them. A new drug that treats the same target as an existing therapy is usually not additive; prefer options that address an untreated aspect of the condition or that intentionally combine mechanisms only when clinically justified."
    },
    {
      "id": "route_and_systemic_exposure",
      "text": "When two options share a drug class, prefer the route or formulation with the lowest systemic exposure that still treats the local or stated problem, especially when systemic therapy carries known class risks. Do not assume one route is always safer; use the stated condition and patient factors to decide."
    },
    {
      "id": "do_not_invent_facts",
      "text": "Only use patient facts that were explicitly stated. A missing laboratory value, test result, or history is not evidence for or against an option. If a decision depends on an unstated fact, say what is needed rather than assuming it is present or absent."
    },
    {
      "id": "separate_indication_from_comorbidity",
      "text": "Use comorbidities and concurrent conditions primarily as safety modifiers and contraindication checks, not as the reason to pick a drug that does not treat the stated condition. The option must first fit the indication, then survive the patient-specific safety screen."
    },
    {
      "id": "class_safety_logic",
      "text": "Before selecting, identify the drug class and its signature risks (bleeding, seizure threshold, cardiac conduction, immunosuppression, hormonal effects, GI ulceration, etc.). Match those risks against the patient's stated conditions and current medicines; eliminate options whose class risk is unacceptable given the stated facts."
    },
    {
      "id": "active_disease_over_background_history",
      "text": "When the case describes an active, worsening problem, prioritize options that treat that active process. Historical conditions that are stable, controlled, or distant should inform safety and monitoring but should not displace treatment of the current active problem."
    },
    {
      "id": "dose_and_monitoring_are_part_of_fit",
      "text": "An option is only appropriate if its expected monitoring and dose adjustment can plausibly be done given the patient's stated situation. If a candidate requires intensive monitoring or dose titration that the case does not support, note that as a reason to prefer an alternative."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "stated active condition",
    "current medicines",
    "drug class risk",
    "route of administration",
    "systemic exposure",
    "contraindication",
    "interaction",
    "indication match",
    "missing fact not present",
    "monitoring requirement"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the working diagnosis, the active problem being treated, all current medicines with their indications, and only explicitly stated patient facts. Mark any unstated fact as unknown and do not use it as if present.",
    "safety_check": "Screen each candidate against stated comorbidities, current medicines, and drug-class risks. Treat stable historical conditions as monitoring modifiers, not as the primary indication. If an option requires an unstated fact to be safe, flag it as unsupported.",
    "option_selection": "Select the option whose indication matches the stated active condition, whose route and systemic exposure fit the patient's situation, whose class risks are acceptable given current medicines and comorbidities, and whose monitoring needs are feasible. Do not choose an option that treats only a symptom, comorbidity, or side effect unless the case explicitly asks for that."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weighted the first plausible indication it recognized and under-used the full patient context: current medicines, comorbidity, and prior therapy. It also treated a drug that is merely present in the history as automatically disqualifying, and sometimes chose a less-supported option when two drugs shared the same indication. The fix is to extract all stated facts, then compare every option against indication, patient-specific safety, and whether the option is the best-supported choice for the stated condition.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "extract_all_stated_facts",
      "text": "Before choosing, list the stated diagnosis or syndrome, every current medicine, relevant past medical history, and any recent or active infection. Do not infer a fact that is not stated."
    },
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "Eliminate options that do not treat the stated condition or syndrome. A drug with no matching indication should not be selected even if it is otherwise familiar or safe."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_med_safety",
      "text": "When a current medicine is named, check whether the candidate option interacts with it or duplicates its effect. If no interaction is stated, do not invent one; if an interaction is stated or well established, prefer an alternative that still treats the condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "comorbidity_and_immunity",
      "text": "Adjust for stated comorbidity and immune status. In immunosuppressed or transplant patients, avoid treatments that could worsen infection, and prefer agents indicated for the suspected infection or for rejection prevention as appropriate."
    },
    {
      "id": "best_supported_among_indicated",
      "text": "When more than one option is indicated for the condition, choose the one best supported for that specific presentation, severity, or patient context rather than the first indicated option."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_contraindicated_or_wrong_class",
      "text": "Reject options from the wrong drug class for the stated problem, and reject options that are contraindicated or likely harmful given the patient's stated condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_benchmark_shortcuts",
      "text": "Do not use case identifiers, answer letters, or position-based shortcuts. Base every choice only on the stated clinical facts and general drug knowledge."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "stated diagnosis",
    "current medicines",
    "comorbidity",
    "immunosuppression",
    "indication",
    "contraindication",
    "drug interaction",
    "best-supported option"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the stated condition or syndrome, all current medicines, relevant past medical history, immune status, and any active or recent infection. Treat only explicitly stated facts as present.",
    "safety_check": "Check each candidate against the patient's stated condition, current medicines, comorbidity, and immune status. Do not invent a missing fact or a missing interaction; do not treat a listed medicine as automatically disqualifying unless the interaction or contraindication is stated or well established.",
    "option_selection": "First eliminate options without the correct indication or from the wrong class. Then, among remaining indicated options, choose the one best supported for the specific presentation and safest for this patient's stated context."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weighted surface drug-class familiarity and under-used the stated diagnosis plus patient-specific facts (comorbidities, interacting medicines, fluid retention, infection severity). It also treated near-equivalent options as interchangeable and sometimes picked a plausible-but-not-indicated drug. Failures cluster around: (1) not anchoring on the named/implied condition, (2) ignoring stated contraindications or interactions, (3) failing to distinguish first-line vs. supportive vs. irrelevant options, and (4) confusing brand/generic equivalents.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "anchor_on_stated_diagnosis",
      "text": "First restate the working diagnosis from the dialogue. Eliminate any option that does not treat that condition, even if it is a common or familiar drug. Only then compare remaining options on safety and patient fit."
    },
    {
      "id": "indication_before_safety",
      "text": "Rank options by whether they are indicated for the stated condition. A drug that is safe but not indicated should lose to an indicated drug unless the indicated drug is contraindicated or clearly unsafe for this patient."
    },
    {
      "id": "patient_specific_contraindications",
      "text": "Explicitly check each candidate against stated comorbidities and current medicines. Do not assume a missing fact is absent; if a key safety fact is not stated, treat it as unknown and prefer the option whose safety does not depend on that unknown."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_and_organ_risk",
      "text": "When a current medicine is named, consider interactions and additive risks (bleeding, sedation, serotonergic effects, renal/hepatic load, blood pressure effects). Prefer options that do not compound the patient's existing problem."
    },
    {
      "id": "first_line_vs_alternative",
      "text": "Distinguish first-line treatment from second-line, adjunctive, or symptomatic options. When two options are in the same class, choose the one that is standard for the specific presentation and avoids the patient's stated risk."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_plausible_but_wrong_class",
      "text": "Do not select a drug merely because it is used somewhere in the same body system. Match the mechanism to the pathophysiology: antibiotics for bacterial infection, replacement hormones for deficiency, immunosuppressants for autoimmune disease, etc."
    },
    {
      "id": "brand_generic_equivalence",
      "text": "Recognize when two options are the same active drug under different names. If both are otherwise appropriate, prefer the option that is the standard formulary name for the indication, but do not treat them as different mechanisms."
    },
    {
      "id": "symptom_cluster_reasoning",
      "text": "Use the full symptom cluster, not a single symptom, to infer the condition. Fatigue plus dizziness on standing plus weight loss suggests adrenal insufficiency; recurrent oral ulcers plus fever suggests aphthous stomatitis; cataplexy plus sleepiness suggests narcolepsy."
    },
    {
      "id": "fluid_and_electrolyte_caution",
      "text": "When fluid retention, edema, hypertension, or kidney stones are stated, avoid drugs that worsen those problems and prefer options that treat the underlying cause without adding fluid or electrolyte burden."
    },
    {
      "id": "infection_treatment_priority",
      "text": "For suspected bacterial infection, an appropriate antibiotic is required; anti-inflammatory or symptomatic drugs are not substitutes. Choose the antibiotic that matches the organism and is safe with the patient's current medicines."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "indicated for the stated diagnosis",
    "first-line vs alternative",
    "patient-specific contraindication",
    "drug interaction",
    "brand-generic equivalence",
    "symptom cluster",
    "fluid retention",
    "antibiotic for bacterial infection"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the working diagnosis, the full symptom cluster, current medicines, comorbidities, and any stated risks (bleeding, fluid retention, renal, cardiac, serotonergic). Mark unknown facts as unknown rather than absent.",
    "safety_check": "For each candidate, check indication first, then contraindications and interactions with stated medicines and comorbidities. Prefer options whose safety does not rely on unstated facts.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option that is both indicated for the stated condition and safest for this patient. Eliminate non-indicated, wrong-class, and risk-compounding options before comparing within-class alternatives."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weighted the most familiar or first-line drug for the stated diagnosis and under-weighted the patient's already-stated medication, prior therapy, and contraindication context. It also sometimes treated a diagnostic or supportive agent as if it were the treatment target, and it failed to distinguish between drug classes and formulations when the indication required a specific route or systemic effect.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "current_medication_first",
      "text": "Before selecting a new medicine, explicitly restate the patient's current medicines and ask whether the proposed option is duplicative, interacting, or already failed. If the patient is already on a drug for the same indication, do not choose that same drug again unless the question is about continuation or optimization."
    },
    {
      "id": "diagnosis_treatment_match",
      "text": "Match the option to the stated diagnosis, not to a symptom or a related condition. If the diagnosis is an infection, choose an anti-infective active against that organism; if it is a hormone deficiency, choose replacement therapy; if it is an inflammatory or immune condition, choose an immunomodulator or corticosteroid appropriate to the severity and route."
    },
    {
      "id": "contraindication_and_immunosuppression",
      "text": "When the patient is immunosuppressed, on chemotherapy, or has a transplant, avoid live vaccines and avoid systemic corticosteroids when they could worsen an active infection. Prefer targeted antimicrobial therapy over broad immunosuppression in suspected herpes-zoster or other active infections."
    },
    {
      "id": "route_and_formulation_matter",
      "text": "Do not treat different formulations or routes as interchangeable. An intra-articular or intralesional corticosteroid is not the same as a systemic corticosteroid for a systemic disease such as hemolytic anemia. Choose the formulation that matches the intended site and systemic need."
    },
    {
      "id": "diagnostic_agent_not_treatment",
      "text": "A diagnostic or imaging agent is not the therapeutic answer unless the question asks for it. If the patient is taking a diagnostic tracer, use it only as context; select the actual treatment for the diagnosed condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_contraindicated_or_irrelevant_options",
      "text": "Eliminate options that are not indicated for the stated condition or that are contraindicated by the patient's current state. Do not choose a drug merely because it is familiar for a related symptom; verify the indication against the working diagnosis."
    },
    {
      "id": "safety_check_without_inventing",
      "text": "Check interactions and contraindications only from facts stated in the case. If a fact is missing, do not assume it is present or absent; instead, note the uncertainty and choose the option that is safest under the stated facts."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "current medication",
    "already taking",
    "contraindicated",
    "immunosuppressed",
    "route",
    "formulation",
    "diagnostic agent",
    "indication"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated condition, all current medicines including any diagnostic or supportive agents, prior failed or ongoing therapy, immune status, and relevant organ function or pregnancy facts. Do not infer missing facts.",
    "safety_check": "Check patient-specific contraindications and interactions using only stated facts. Flag immunosuppression, active infection, renal or hepatic impairment, and current drugs that overlap with the proposed option. Do not invent absent facts.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option that best satisfies the working diagnosis and the patient-specific safety context. Prefer targeted therapy over symptomatic or unrelated therapy, avoid duplicating a current medicine, and match route and formulation to the intended effect."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "Failures came from anchoring on a superficially plausible comorbidity or drug-mechanism story instead of the stated diagnosis-plus-safety logic: indications were sometimes overridden by non-contraindications (e.g., a tolerable or unspecified interaction), and an answer was selected that either lacked the indicated mechanism or was actually contraindicated/less appropriate for the stated condition. Two cases also exposed a conflict between the prior evidence and the stated diagnosis, where the selected option matched a secondary feature rather than the primary indication.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "When the stated diagnosis is clear, first identify the drug class specifically indicated for that diagnosis; only then use patient-specific safety to refine the choice. Do not let a secondary concern displace the indicated mechanism unless that concern is an actual stated contraindication."
    },
    {
      "id": "real_not_assumed_contraindication",
      "text": "Treat only explicitly stated contraindications, allergies, interactions, or organ-function limits as barriers. A comorbidity that is not explicitly declared incompatible does not by itself eliminate an otherwise indicated drug."
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_mismatch",
      "text": "Reject options whose mechanism does not plausibly treat the stated condition, even if the option is familiar or has some unrelated proposed benefit. Symptomatic or supportive drugs are not substitutes for disease-directed therapy unless no indicated option exists."
    },
    {
      "id": "conflict_resolution",
      "text": "If prior evidence conflicts with the stated diagnosis, resolve by the stated diagnosis and the option that directly treats it; do not select a drug merely because it aligns with a secondary or incidental feature."
    },
    {
      "id": "comorbidity_priority",
      "text": "When the diagnosis implies a choice among otherwise indicated drugs, prefer the option that is both indicated and least likely to aggravate a stated comorbidity (e.g., vasospastic disease, hyperthyroidism, cardiac disease). Only stated, relevant comorbidities should drive this preference."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_handling",
      "text": "Do not invent a missing patient fact to justify or reject an option. If a contraindication or interaction is absent from the case, do not treat it as present; base the choice on stated indication and stated safety facts only."
    },
    {
      "id": "secondary_benefit_check",
      "text": "When two options are both plausibly indicated, compare mechanism of action, contraindication risk from stated facts, and whether one option better addresses the primary condition rather than only a secondary symptom."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "stated diagnosis",
    "indicated mechanism",
    "explicit contraindication",
    "stated comorbidity",
    "primary vs secondary indication",
    "no invented facts"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the stated diagnosis, current medicines, explicitly stated allergies/contraindications, and relevant comorbidities. Mark unstated facts as absent rather than inferred.",
    "safety_check": "Use only explicitly stated contraindications, interactions, and comorbidity-related risks. Do not assume a risk from a drug class or missing laboratory value unless the case states it.",
    "option_selection": "Select the option whose mechanism directly treats the stated diagnosis while respecting stated safety facts. If multiple options are indicated, prefer the one least likely to worsen a stated relevant comorbidity or interact with current therapy."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent often confuses a relevant prior medication or comorbidity with the indication for the current problem, selects a drug that is less appropriate because of an unstated assumption, and fails to distinguish first-line disease-specific therapy from supportive or non-indicated options.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "Before comparing safety, confirm that each candidate is actually indicated for the diagnosed condition; exclude options that treat unrelated systems or have no plausible disease match."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_medication_context",
      "text": "When the patient reports a current medicine, use it only as context for interactions, contraindications, or prior treatment response; do not let it replace the indication for the new problem unless the case explicitly asks about that medicine."
    },
    {
      "id": "prior_treatment_response",
      "text": "If the patient says a drug for the same problem is not working or is not working well, favor an alternative in the same indicated class rather than repeating the same agent or choosing an unrelated drug."
    },
    {
      "id": "organ_safety_preference",
      "text": "When the patient has a stated organ-related concern such as prior liver enzyme elevation, prefer an indicated option with a safer organ-specific profile and avoid assuming that all drugs in the class carry identical risk."
    },
    {
      "id": "infection_organism_match",
      "text": "For suspected infections, match the antimicrobial spectrum to the stated organism or syndrome; do not select antibacterials for fungal syndromes or antifungals for bacterial syndromes unless the case gives a mixed or unclear picture."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_not_present",
      "text": "Treat allergies, intolerances, renal failure, pregnancy, or other disqualifying facts as absent unless they are explicitly stated; do not invent a contraindication to justify an option."
    },
    {
      "id": "mutation_or_specialty_target",
      "text": "When a diagnosis includes a molecular subtype, receptor status, or other targeted qualifier, prefer the drug that matches that qualifier and exclude otherwise active drugs that lack the required target or indication."
    },
    {
      "id": "complement_disorder_target",
      "text": "For complement-mediated disorders, recognize that terminal complement inhibitors are disease-directed, while immunomodulators or stimulants are not substitutes unless the case explicitly supports them."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_benchmark_shortcuts",
      "text": "Do not encode case identifiers, option letters, or answer-position heuristics; base each selection only on the stated diagnosis, patient facts, and drug properties."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "indicated for the stated diagnosis",
    "prior treatment failure",
    "organ-specific safety",
    "organism match",
    "molecular target",
    "missing fact not present",
    "avoid non-indicated supportive drugs"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the active diagnosis, current medicines with indication and response, prior organ or infection history, and any molecular or syndrome qualifiers. Do not transform a historical medication or comorbidity into the indication for the current problem.",
    "safety_check": "Use only stated patient facts to assess contraindications and interactions. Do not invent missing allergies, organ failure, pregnancy, or intolerance. Consider whether a prior organ concern or current medicine narrows the safer choice within an indicated class.",
    "option_selection": "First eliminate options without an indication for the stated diagnosis. Then prefer the option that best matches syndrome, organism, molecular target, prior treatment response, and patient-specific safety. If the patient already failed a drug for the same problem, choose a different indicated alternative rather than repeating it."
  }
}

